Karole: bug fixes, in-memory playlist, blacklist, cookie login, song length limit, faster player, admin controls

Apply from the repository root, on top of commit 69553ac ("broken stub"):

    git apply karole-fixes.patch

This description is ignored by git apply; the changes start at the first file header below.


BUG FIXES

Frontend
- Public playlist page crashed on load: loadPlaylst() typo in src/app/playlist/page.js.
- Pause did not hold in the admin player. The autoplay effect in src/app/admin/view/page.js
  depended on the song object, which is a new object on every 500ms poll, so it kept
  calling play() and forcing playing=true. It is removed; the existing play/pause effect
  now also depends on the song id so a newly loaded song still starts.
- FastAPI validation errors (422) were shown as "[object Object]" because "detail" is a
  list there. New helper src/lib/api-error.js, used by the add page and admin names page.
- adminFetch logged every request header to the console, including the bearer token.
  (adminFetch is rewritten for the cookie login below, so the debug logging is gone.)
- "Move" with an empty position box sent order_num: null. Added the same integer check
  the swap button already has.

Backend
- Valid YouTube links were rejected: share links (youtu.be/ID?si=...), links with &t=42s,
  and watch?...&v=ID. The last one was a stray ";" in the regex ("&;v="). The regex now
  also allows extra parameters after the video id.
- watch?v=ID&list=... links made yt-dlp return the whole playlist, so the stored title and
  channel were the playlist's and playback found no stream. Added "noplaylist": True.
- Adding a song returned 500 when the channel icon could not be fetched (None is not a
  str) or the channel name was longer than 30 characters. url_channel_icon is now
  optional and url_creator allows 100 characters.
- Login returned 500 for non-ASCII usernames or passwords: hmac.compare_digest raises
  TypeError on non-ASCII str. It now compares bytes and returns a normal 401.
- /names/blacklist, /names/unblacklist and /names/to_alt returned the document from
  before the update. They now pass return_document=AFTER.


BEHAVIOUR CHANGES

Playlist is no longer stored in MongoDB
- New file backend/playlist/playlist_store.py holds a Playlist class: the songs in play
  order, the current song and the playing flag. One instance lives for the life of the
  server. Songs are still PlaylistEntry objects, so the API responses keep their shape.
- order_num is simply the position in the list and is renumbered after every change, so
  it cannot have gaps or duplicates. The retry loops, negative order_num tricks and the
  playlist indexes in database.py are gone.
- app.state.current_song is gone; playback state lives in the Playlist object.
- Every change appends one line to backend/playlist/playlist_log.jsonl (gitignored): a
  JSON object with a timestamp, the playing flag, the current song's p_id and all songs.
  Nothing is read back at startup, so a restart starts with an empty playlist. The last
  line is enough to rebuild the object by hand. Each line holds the full playlist, so
  the file grows quickly on a busy night.
- The old "playlist" collection in MongoDB is no longer used and can be dropped.

Skip and Back
- They used the order_num cached when the song started. After a reorder or delete that
  number was stale, so skip could replay the current song or jump over one. The current
  song is now tracked as the object itself and its position is looked up when needed.
- Deleting the playing song moves playback to the song that took its place, or stops if
  there is none. Flush resets playback.

Where a new song is placed
- Previously a first-time singer's song was inserted after the last priority-1 song even
  when playback was already past that point, so it was never reached.
- New rule: if the playing song's priority_num is greater than the new song's, the new
  song is placed right after the playing song. Otherwise placement is unchanged.
- Known consequence: several late joiners during the same song stack newest-first.

Matching a pending operation (/operation/handle)
- It used to delete the alternate name's songs, then merge the names, then re-add each
  song through add_song, which re-fetched YouTube. A missing primary name or one failed
  fetch lost songs. It now merges the names first (that fails before changing anything)
  and then re-queues the songs in memory. The playing song is renamed in place.
- pending_operation.py no longer touches a playlist collection. The commented-out
  "Forgive mode" block was removed because it was entirely playlist database calls; it is
  still in git history.

Blacklist
- /playlist/add now returns 403 "This name is blacklisted" when the name, or the primary
  name it is an alternate of, is blacklisted. The field was stored but never checked.

Cookie login (replaces the token in sessionStorage)
- POST /auth/login now sets an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie "karole_admin_token" that
  lasts two hours, and no longer returns the token in the body. require_admin reads that
  cookie; the Authorization: Bearer header is no longer accepted. New route:
  POST /auth/logout, because only the server can delete an HttpOnly cookie.
- /playlist/playback-stream uses the cookie too, so the token is no longer put in the
  video URL.
- Frontend: adminFetch sends credentials and goes to /admin/login on any 401, which also
  covers an expired login. The per-page sessionStorage checks are gone. The admin playlist
  page loads /playlist/current-play on each refresh; that route needs a login, which the
  public playlist route does not.
- src/lib/config.js now builds BACKEND_URI from the page's own host name (port 8000). A
  SameSite cookie is only sent between matching hosts, so a page opened as localhost:3000
  must talk to localhost:8000, not 127.0.0.1:8000. Both origins are already in the CORS
  list.
- The guest's saved name on the add page moved from localStorage to a one-year cookie.
  Names saved before this change are not carried over.

Song length limit
- New environment variable MAX_SONG_LENGTH_SECONDS, default 600. It is not added to
  backend/.env by this patch; add the line there to change it.
- Long songs are still added. PlaylistEntry gains "duration" (seconds, from yt-dlp, may be
  null for live streams) and "is_too_long".
- The admin playlist page shows a notice for every flagged song with two buttons:
  "Skip song" removes it through the existing /playlist/delete (if it is playing, playback
  moves to the next song), and "Keep" calls the new POST /playlist/keep?playlist_id=...
  which clears the flag so the notice does not come back.

Playback controls on the admin playlist page
- The page now has Back, Play/Pause and Skip buttons and a line showing what is playing.
  They call the same /playlist/back, /play_pause and /skip routes as the player page.
- The state is refreshed after every action and with the 5 second playlist refresh, so a
  song change made on the player page can take up to 5 seconds to show here.

Player lag
- Measured on one 4K video: the yt-dlp lookup took about 3 seconds on every stream
  request, and "bestvideo+bestaudio" picked 2160p AV1 at about 9 Mbit/s.
- The format is now capped to 1080p H.264 with AAC audio (about 3 Mbit/s for the same
  video), which browsers decode in hardware.
- Stream lookups are cached in memory by song URL for one hour, so replays and repeated
  browser requests skip the lookup. When a song starts streaming, the next song is looked
  up in the background so it starts without the wait. Failed lookups are not cached.

Event loop
- The yt-dlp lookups and the channel icon download ran synchronously inside async routes
  and froze every other request for seconds. They now run through asyncio.to_thread.
- ffmpeg's stderr was piped but never read; it now goes to DEVNULL.


TESTING

- Done, with fakes: a script drove the real FastAPI app through 68 checks with the names
  lookup, yt-dlp and ffmpeg inputs replaced by fakes. It covered URL validation, add,
  blacklist, duplicate, move, swap, delete, skip, back, jump, play/pause, flush, the
  re-queue on match, the log file, the play-next rule, cookie login and logout, tampered
  cookies, CORS with credentials for both origins, the length limit with Skip and Keep,
  and the lookup cache, prefetch and expiry.
- Done, for real: the server was run over HTTP with real yt-dlp and ffmpeg (only the names
  lookup faked). A real share link was added in 3.9s. The stream from
  /playlist/playback-stream, authenticated by the cookie alone, was H.264 1080p with AAC.
  Time to the first byte went from 3.6s to 0.6s for a repeated request and for the
  prefetched next song.
- eslint reports no errors and next build passes.
- Not done: nothing was run against a real MongoDB, so the names endpoints and the full
  /operation/handle route are untested end to end. Nothing was tried in a browser: the
  player fix, the cookie being sent with fetch and with the <video> request, the new
  notice and the new control buttons all still need a manual check.


NOT PART OF THIS PATCH

- backend/.env is committed in this public repository with ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD
  and JWT_SECRET. Anyone can log in as admin or forge a login cookie. The values need
  rotating, and the file should be untracked (git rm --cached backend/.env) and gitignored.
- When the playlist runs out, the current song becomes empty and the next Play starts
  again from the first song.
- The cookie is not marked Secure because the app runs over plain http. If it is ever
  served over https, add secure=True in backend/auth.py.
- Existing files were not reformatted, to keep this diff readable.

